#!/usr/bin/env sh

# Script: immediate-lock
# Purpose: Lock the screen immediately (single entry point for all lock triggers)
# Requirements: Arch Linux, quickshell OR hyprlock, flock (util-linux)
#
# Used by Super+L, wlogout, the system menu, and hypridle's lock_cmd.
#
# Two routes, chosen at RUNTIME rather than by a template branch: the Quickshell
# lock surface (§22) when the shell answers, hyprlock otherwise. Runtime on
# purpose -- if the shell is down, its restart budget is spent, or its QML tree
# will not load, Super+L must still lock the screen. `grace` is a hyprlock CLI
# flag since 0.9.6, no longer a config key.
#
# 🚨 `quickshell ipc call` exits 0 EVEN WHEN IT FAILS: a target that does not
# exist prints "Target not found." and still returns 0. So the route is decided
# by the ANSWER, never the exit status -- the same rule quickshell-toggle's
# shell_running() follows. Anything but "ok" falls through to hyprlock,
# "missing-pam" included: hyprlock reads the same /etc/pam.d/hyprlock and will
# report the real problem in its own UI rather than leaving the screen unlocked.
#
# 🚨 The re-entrancy guard is a LOCK FILE, not `pidof hyprlock`. Since
# hypr/conf/general sets misc:allow_session_lock_restore, the compositor accepts
# a second locker where it used to refuse one -- so a check-then-act guard that
# loses its race now displaces a live lock screen out from under whoever is
# typing into it. flock closes that window: the descriptor is inherited by the
# exec'd hyprlock and released only when it exits. On the Quickshell route it is
# released as this script exits, which is correct -- the shell's own lock state
# is the guard from there on, and a second call just gets "ok" back.
#
# That same compositor option is what makes a STRANDED lock recoverable: an
# ext-session-lock outlives its client, so a killed locker leaves the session
# behind Hyprland's failsafe. The lock file is gone with the dead process, so
# this script proceeds and the new locker re-acquires the existing lock. Ask
# `desktop/session-locked` whether that is the situation; it is not consulted
# here because the action is the same either way.

LOCKFILE="${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/run/user/$(id -u)}/immediate-lock.lock"

exec 9>"$LOCKFILE" || exit 1
flock -n 9 || exit 0

# 🚨 Bounded: a shell that is alive but hung never answers, and without the
# timeout this blocked forever while holding the flock -- no hyprlock, every
# later lock attempt exiting 0 on the held lock, and hypridle's inhibit_sleep=3
# only holding suspend for logind's InhibitDelayMaxSec (5 s), after which the
# machine sleeps UNLOCKED. 1 s, not more: a healthy answer takes ~35 ms, and
# hyprlock needs the rest of that 5 s to map at 4K. If the shell later resumes
# it still runs the queued call; with allow_session_lock_restore its lock then
# replaces hyprlock's rather than stacking on it.
if [ "$(timeout 1 quickshell -c dotfiles ipc call lock lock 2>/dev/null)" = ok ]; then
    exit 0
fi

exec hyprlock --grace 0
